 "lq": [
  {
   "_id": 18097,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"2462\" data-start=\"2233\">In Unix/Linux operating systems, a new process is created using the <strong data-end=\"2313\" data-start=\"2301\"><code data-end=\"2311\" data-start=\"2303\">fork()</code></strong> system call. It duplicates the calling process and creates a <strong data-end=\"2392\" data-start=\"2375\">child process</strong>, which runs independently but initially shares the same memory space.</p>\n\n<h3 data-end=\"2497\" data-start=\"2469\"><strong data-end=\"2497\" data-start=\"2473\">Working of <code data-end=\"2494\" data-start=\"2486\">fork()</code>:</strong></h3>\n\n<ul data-end=\"2645\" data-start=\"2498\">\n\t<li data-end=\"2539\" data-start=\"2498\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2539\" data-start=\"2500\">Returns <strong data-end=\"2513\" data-start=\"2508\">0</strong> to the <strong data-end=\"2530\" data-start=\"2521\">child</strong> process.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"2601\" data-start=\"2540\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2601\" data-start=\"2542\">Returns the <strong data-end=\"2574\" data-start=\"2554\">PID of the child</strong> to the <strong data-end=\"2592\" data-start=\"2582\">parent</strong> process.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"2645\" data-start=\"2602\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2645\" data-start=\"2604\">Returns <strong data-end=\"2618\" data-start=\"2612\">-1</strong> if process creation fails.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<pre data-end=\"2801\" data-start=\"2647\">\n\n&nbsp;</pre>\n\n<p><code>pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { // Child process } else if (pid &gt; 0) { // Parent process } else { // Error in creating process } </code></p>\n\n<h3 data-end=\"2827\" data-start=\"2808\"><strong data-end=\"2825\" data-start=\"2812\">getpid():</strong></h3>\n\n<p data-end=\"2945\" data-start=\"2828\">This function returns the <strong data-end=\"2868\" data-start=\"2854\">Process ID</strong> of the current process. It is useful for identifying and managing processes.</p>\n\n<pre data-end=\"2992\" data-start=\"2947\">\n<code>printf(&quot;Child PID: %d\\n&quot;, getpid()); </code></pre>\n\n<h3 data-end=\"3019\" data-start=\"2999\"><strong data-end=\"3017\" data-start=\"3003\">getppid():</strong></h3>\n\n<p data-end=\"3107\" data-start=\"3020\">Returns the <strong data-end=\"3053\" data-start=\"3032\">Parent Process ID</strong>. Useful for checking who created the current process.</p>\n\n<pre data-end=\"3159\" data-start=\"3109\">\n<code>printf(&quot;My Parent PID: %d\\n&quot;, getppid()); </code></pre>\n\n<h3 data-end=\"3183\" data-start=\"3166\"><strong data-end=\"3181\" data-start=\"3170\">wait():</strong></h3>\n\n<p data-end=\"3313\" data-start=\"3184\">The <code data-end=\"3196\" data-start=\"3188\">wait()</code> system call makes the <strong data-end=\"3234\" data-start=\"3219\">parent wait</strong> until the child process <strong data-end=\"3273\" data-start=\"3259\">terminates</strong>, helping to avoid <strong data-end=\"3312\" data-start=\"3292\">zombie processes</strong>.</p>\n\n<pre data-end=\"3371\" data-start=\"3315\">\n<code>wait(NULL); // Waits for any child to terminate </code></pre>\n\n<h3 data-end=\"3399\" data-start=\"3378\"><strong data-end=\"3399\" data-start=\"3382\">Example Code:</strong></h3>\n\n<pre data-end=\"3847\" data-start=\"3401\">\n\n&nbsp;</pre>\n\n<p><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt; #include &lt;unistd.h&gt; #include &lt;sys/wait.h&gt; int main() { pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { // Child process printf(&quot;Child Process: PID = %d, PPID = %d\\n&quot;, getpid(), getppid()); } else if (pid &gt; 0) { // Parent process wait(NULL); // Wait for child printf(&quot;Parent Process: PID = %d\\n&quot;, getpid()); } else { printf(&quot;Fork failed!\\n&quot;); } return 0; } </code></p>\n\n<h3 data-end=\"3873\" data-start=\"3854\"><strong data-end=\"3873\" data-start=\"3858\">Conclusion:</strong></h3>\n\n<ul data-end=\"4138\" data-start=\"3875\">\n\t<li data-end=\"3916\" data-start=\"3875\">\n\t<p data-end=\"3916\" data-start=\"3877\"><code data-end=\"3885\" data-start=\"3877\">fork()</code> is key for process creation.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"3976\" data-start=\"3917\">\n\t<p data-end=\"3976\" data-start=\"3919\"><code data-end=\"3929\" data-start=\"3919\">getpid()</code> and <code data-end=\"3945\" data-start=\"3934\">getppid()</code> help track process identity.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"4138\" data-start=\"3977\">\n\t<p data-end=\"4138\" data-start=\"3979\"><code data-end=\"3987\" data-start=\"3979\">wait()</code> ensures the parent waits for its child, preventing resource leaks.<br data-end=\"4057\" data-start=\"4054\" />\n\tThese functions form the <strong data-end=\"4115\" data-start=\"4082\">foundation of process control</strong> in Unix/Linux systems.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC202J",
   "LEVEL": 3,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO3",
    "PLO4"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p><strong data-end=\"2209\" data-start=\"2047\">Explain how process creation is done using <code data-end=\"2100\" data-start=\"2092\">fork()</code> in Unix/Linux systems. Describe the role of <code data-end=\"2155\" data-start=\"2145\">getpid()</code>, <code data-end=\"2168\" data-start=\"2157\">getppid()</code>, and <code data-end=\"2182\" data-start=\"2174\">wait()</code> functions with examples.</strong></p>\n",
